    labs: "Hb 10.5 g/dL. WBC 4.2. Platelets 85 (thrombocytopenia). Na+ 126 mEq/L (hyponatraemia). K+ 3.1 mEq/L. Creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (AKI). Total bilirubin 4.8 mg/dL (direct 2.5). AST 85, ALT 55 (AST>ALT). Albumin 2.4 g/dL. INR 1.7. Ascitic fluid: SAAG >1.1, PMN <250 cells/mm3 (no SBP). Ammonia: 95 µmol/L.",
    imaging: "Abdominal ultrasound: Coarse nodular liver, caudate lobe hypertrophy, splenomegaly (14.5cm), large volume ascites, portal vein diameter 14mm (portal hypertension).",
    diagnosis: "Decompensated Alcohol-Related Cirrhosis (Child-Pugh Class C) with Ascites, Hepatic Encephalopathy (Grade 2), and Acute Kidney Injury (Hepatorenal Syndrome Type 1 vs Pre-Renal)",
    ddx: ["Decompensated Cirrhosis", "Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis", "Acute Hepatitis", "Heart Failure", "Budd-Chiari Syndrome"],
    goals: "Reverse hepatic encephalopathy, mobilise ascites, prevent SBP, assess for liver transplant, correct hyponatraemia and AKI",
    pharm: "1. Hepatic Encephalopathy: Restart Lactulose 30mL every 2-4 hours until 2-3 soft stools/day. Add Rifaximin 550mg BID (if available). 2. Ascites: Therapeutic paracentesis (large volume if tense). Hold diuretics if creatinine rising or hyponatraemia worsening. IV Albumin 25% 20-40g daily if large volume paracentesis >5L or Hepatorenal Syndrome suspected. 3. AKI: Hold diuretics, volume expand with IV Albumin, stop nephrotoxins. 4. Correct K+ to >3.5. 5. Avoid sedatives for confusion (may worsen encephalopathy).",
    nonPharm: "Low sodium diet (<2g/day). Fluid restriction 1.5L/day if Na <125. Bed rest. Daily weights. Abstain from alcohol completely. Nutritional support (high calorie, moderate protein, with branch-chain amino acids if protein intolerant).",
    carePlan: "Admit to medical ward. Aggressive lactulose therapy. Stop diuretics. Large volume paracentesis with albumin cover. Monitor neuro status, urine output, daily weights, abdominal girth. Discuss transplant evaluation with hepatology. Assess for alcohol cessation programmes.",
    dtps: "1. Decompensated cirrhosis with multiple complications: encephalopathy, ascites, hyponatraemia, AKI. 2. Non-adherence to lactulose precipitating hepatic encephalopathy. 3. Diuretic-induced complications (hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia, AKI).",
    monitoring: "Daily: weight, abdominal girth, strict I/O, neuro status (GCS, asterixis), BMP (Na, K, Cr), LFTs, INR. Monitor stool frequency on lactulose (target: 2-3 soft BMs/day). Monitor for SBP (fever, worsening ascites, abdominal pain).",
    counselling: "Abstinence from alcohol is essential for any improvement in liver function. Lactulose titration: adjust dose to achieve 2-3 soft bowel movements daily. Watch for confusion, sleep reversal, personality changes — early signs of encephalopathy. Low salt diet means no added salt, avoid processed foods.",
    followUp: "Hepatology follow-up in 1-2 weeks post-discharge. Transplant evaluation if eligible (abstinence for 6 months required in most programmes). OGD for variceal screening if not up to date.",
    pearls: "Hepatic encephalopathy is a clinical diagnosis — ammonia levels correlate poorly with severity. Lactulose works by acidifying the colon, converting NH3 to NH4+ (non-absorbable). Rifaximin is an evidence-based add-on for secondary prevention. Diuretics in cirrhosis can precipitate hepatorenal syndrome — use cautiously. Persistent hyponatraemia (Na <120) despite fluid restriction may require 3% saline (risk of osmotic demyelination, correct slowly).",
    references: ["EASL Guidelines: Decompensated Cirrhosis", "AASLD Guidelines: Hepatic Encephalopathy", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Liver Disease"],
