    labs: "Serum Lipase: 1850 U/L (normal <60). Amylase: 1200 U/L. WBC 14.5. CRP 240 mg/L. LFTs: AST 120, ALT 95, ALP 180, GGT 210, Total bilirubin 1.8 mg/dL (suggests possible gallstone in common bile duct). Ca: 8.8 mg/dL. Triglycerides: normal. ABG: pH 7.35. Lactic acid: 2.1.",
    imaging: "Abdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones in a thick-walled gallbladder. Common bile duct 10mm (dilated). Pancreas: diffusely enlarged and hypoechoic (acute pancreatitis). No pseudocyst.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Biliary Pancreatitis (Moderately Severe per Revised Atlanta Classification)",
    ddx: ["Acute Pancreatitis (biliary)", "Acute Pancreatitis (alcoholic)", "Perforated Peptic Ulcer", "Cholecystitis", "Mesenteric Ischaemia"],
    goals: "Pain control, aggressive IV fluid resuscitation, monitor for complications (necrosis, organ failure), definitive management of gallstones",
    pharm: "1. Aggressive IV fluid resuscitation: Ringer's Lactate at 250-300 mL/hour for first 24-48 hours (monitor for fluid overload). 2. Analgesia: IV Morphine 5-10mg q4h PRN (no evidence that morphine worsens outcomes). 3. Antiemetics: Ondansetron 4mg IV q8h. 4. Notify for ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) within 24-48 hours if evidence of cholangitis or persistent biliary obstruction (bilirubin rising, dilated CBD). 5. No routine antibiotics unless evidence of infected necrosis or cholangitis (imipenem or meropenem if infected).",
    nonPharm: "NPO initially (nasogastric tube if vomiting). Stepwise reintroduction of oral feeds when pain subsides, hunger returns, and lipase trending down (start clear liquids, low-fat diet). Monitor for complications: pancreatic necrosis, pseudocyst, organ failure.",
    carePlan: "Admit to ward or HDU. Aggressive IV fluids. Pain control. Monitor for organ failure (renal, respiratory, cardiovascular). Assess severity: APACHE II, Ranson criteria, or BISAP score. Arrange ECRP if biliary obstruction persists. Once pancreatitis resolves, plan for laparoscopic cholecystectomy during same admission (reduces recurrence).",
    dtps: "1. Acute biliary pancreatitis requiring fluid resuscitation and supportive care. 2. Obstructing gallstone requiring ECRP. 3. Risk of necrotising pancreatitis and multi-organ failure.",
    monitoring: "First 24 hours: vital signs q1h, I/O q1h, oxygen saturation, urine output (>0.5 mL/kg/hr). Daily: LFTs, lipase, CRP, Ca, Lactic acid. Assess for organ failure: respiratory (PaO2/FiO2), renal (creatinine), cardiovascular (need for vasopressors). CT scan with contrast at 72 hours if no improvement (to assess for necrosis).",
    counselling: "Acute pancreatitis is a serious condition requiring intensive monitoring. The initial treatment is aggressive IV fluids and strict bowel rest. Gallstones are the most common cause in Kenya — you will need gallbladder removal surgery once the inflammation resolves to prevent recurrence. Recovery may take 1-2 weeks.",
    followUp: "Surgical review for laparoscopic cholecystectomy after resolution. Outpatient follow-up in 4 weeks post-discharge. Pancreatic enzyme replacement (Creon) if exocrine insufficiency develops.",
    pearls: "Revised Atlanta Classification: Mild (no organ failure, no complications), Moderately Severe (organ failure <48 hours or local complications), Severe (organ failure >48 hours). Aggressive IV fluids in first 24 hours are the most important intervention — hypovolaemia is the main driver of pancreatic necrosis. Morphine is safe for pain in pancreatitis. ECRP is indicated only if cholangitis or persistent obstruction.",
    references: ["IAP/APA Guidelines: Acute Pancreatitis", "ACG Guidelines: Acute Pancreatitis", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Gallstone Disease"],
