    medHx: "Mesalazine 4.8g daily (oral, suspended 2 months ago), occasional loperamide for diarrhoea",
    allergies: "Penicillin (rash)",
    pe: "Mildly pale. Abdomen: left lower quadrant tenderness, no guarding, no masses. Perianal inspection: normal. PR: blood on glove. Temperature: 37.5°C.",
    vitals: "HR: 111 bpm, BP: 79/41 mmHg, RR: 36 breaths/min, Temp: 39.7°C, SpO2: 88%",
    labs: "Hb 10.2 g/dL (normocytic, normochromic). WBC 11.5. Platelets 450 (thrombocytosis of inflammation). CRP 65 mg/L. ESR 45 mm/hr. Calprotectin (faecal): 1200 mcg/g (markedly elevated). Albumin 3.0 g/dL. Stool cultures: negative for pathogens. pANCA: positive.",
    imaging: "Flexible sigmoidoscopy: Erythema, granularity, friable mucosa, loss of vascular pattern, superficial ulcerations extending to 40 cm (moderate disease, Mayo Endoscopic Score 2). Biopsies: active chronic colitis with crypt abscesses, no dysplasia.",
    diagnosis: "Moderate Ulcerative Colitis Flare (Pancolitis, Mayo Score 8/12)",
    ddx: ["Ulcerative Colitis", "Crohns Colitis", "Infectious Colitis", "Irritable Bowel Syndrome", "Diverticulitis"],
    goals: "Induce remission, prevent hospitalisation, avoid corticosteroids (steroid-sparing strategy), maintain long-term remission",
    pharm: "1. Re-start and optimise Mesalazine: Mesalazine 4.8g daily PO (maximum dose) + Mesalazine enema 4g/60mL at bedtime for 4 weeks. 2. If inadequate response at 2 weeks: add oral Prednisolone 40mg daily for 1 week, then taper by 5mg weekly. 3. If steroid-dependent or refractory: start Thiopurine (Azathioprine 2-2.5 mg/kg daily or 6-Mercaptopurine 1-1.5 mg/kg daily). 4. If not responding: escalate to biologic therapy (Infliximab 5mg/kg IV at weeks 0, 2, 6 then q8w). 5. Iron supplementation: IV Iron sucrose or oral Ferrous sulphate.",
    nonPharm: "Bowel rest only if severe. Encourage oral nutrition with soft, low-residue diet during acute flare. Avoid NSAIDs (can worsen IBD). Smoking cessation (protective in UC but harmful in Crohns).",
    carePlan: "Admit if severe (Mayo >10, Hb <9, albumin <3, >10 bloody stools/day) or if outpatient management fails. For this moderate flare: outpatient optimised oral + rectal mesalazine. Review in 2 weeks. If no improvement: start prednisolone taper. Consider thiopurine for steroid-sparing maintenance.",
    dtps: "1. Active moderate UC flare requiring escalation of mesalazine therapy. 2. Relapse due to medication non-adherence (cost barrier). 3. Risk of steroid dependence given multiple prior steroid courses.",
    monitoring: "Stool chart: frequency, consistency, blood. CRP trend. FBC for Hb, platelets. Monitor for steroid side effects if prescribed. If on thiopurine: TPMT genotype before starting, then monthly FBC and LFTs. If on Infliximab: TB screening (Quantiferon, CXR) and HBV serology.",
    counselling: "Ulcerative colitis is a chronic condition requiring long-term maintenance therapy — even when you feel well. Stopping medication is the most common cause of flares. Mesalazine maintenance therapy reduces colorectal cancer risk. Report any increase in bloody stools, severe abdominal pain, or fever immediately.",
    followUp: "Gastroenterology clinic in 2 weeks to assess response. If in remission, step down to mesalazine 2.4g daily maintenance. Surveillance colonoscopy after 8-10 years of disease for dysplasia/cancer surveillance.",
    pearls: "Mayo Score: 0-2 remission, 3-5 mild, 6-10 moderate, 11-12 severe. Pancolitis warrants full maintenance therapy — left-sided disease can use mesalazine enemas alone. Infliximab has transformed UC management — early use in steroid-refractory disease reduces colectomy rates. Mesalazine works locally on the colonic mucosa — never crush/chew tablets (need the pH-dependent coating to reach the colon intact).",
    references: ["ECCO Guidelines: Ulcerative Colitis", "ACG Guidelines: Ulcerative Colitis Management", "WHO: Essential IBD Medicines"],
